#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114927 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "b13213ea-be0a-5f1a-85fd-02b69556bd58" :question/math-academy-id "e-3537" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $0^\\circ \\leq x <  360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution. However, we can generate solutions by adding integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =x+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘} \\\\  \\\\ {x}_{2} & =x+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=150^\\circ, 330^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1 \\cdot x_2$ is $150 \\cdot 330 = 49\\,500.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114928 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "a30eea56-2088-549f-a986-08d54117615b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3539" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\cot x + 3\\sqrt{3}=0$ for $-180^\\circ <                      x <                      180^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=-{30}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-30^\\circ$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 =-30^\\circ .$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{30}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x= -30^\\circ, 150^\\circ .$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n-30\\cdot(150) = -4\\,500.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114970 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "bc14e14d-d58f-5556-af4d-4aa52f394e58" :question/math-academy-id "e-4266" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation  $2\\cot x =9 - \\cot x$ for $0 \\leq x <                         2\\pi,$ what is the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2,$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =9 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\cot{x}=\\dfrac{1}{\\tan{x}}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\tan{x} =\\dfrac{1}{3} .\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{3})=0.321\\,750≈0.321\\,8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $0.321\\,8$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 0.321\\,8.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\pi +0.321\\,8 \\\\  & =3.463\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is\n\n$$\n0.321\\,8 \\cdot 3.463\\,4 \\approx 1.1\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115055 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "9344fc13-add4-57bc-9558-73cc0b95fea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7699" :question/problem "Given that $x_1$ and $x_2$ are the solutions to the equation $3- 2\\cot x =3$ for $0^\\circ <                           x <                           360^\\circ,$ what is the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\cot x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{1}{0} = \\textrm{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\cot x = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\cos x &=0\\[5pt]\nx &= \\arccos 0 \\[5pt]\nx &= 90^\\circ\n}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is $x_1 = 90^\\circ.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $180^\\circ$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=90^\\circ, 270^\\circ.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of $x_1\\cdot x_2$ is \n\n$$\n90 \\cdot(270) = 24\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278914 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97c8fa7e-edde-5220-bc7d-9c70b74fd26b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278916} {:answer/id #uuid "47d4762a-9733-5065-84d9-079e643a2da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278917} {:answer/id #uuid "8df980c9-1d81-50d8-aa17-16c27741f4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278918} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0176d8-6ce8-5202-a8b8-f9cb9fb8be47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278919} {:answer/id #uuid "d6fae503-fba0-5873-b5c4-2f973e211d34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8df980c9-1d81-50d8-aa17-16c27741f4e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278918} :answer-field/id #uuid "72186251-acfd-58ae-8b3d-240e90cefaf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4bfe25b-7745-56bc-a188-ffde09186b7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120620" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot \\frac{7\\pi }{6}=\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b767c32-42b4-56d9-86cf-1ed8de82400b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278923} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe0bb7c-7591-5493-9388-ce809211c419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278924} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7ae4af-ccf0-5d23-9379-0a17a4b9879c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278925} {:answer/id #uuid "b4789114-b6e4-5b3e-bf5d-f3d13dcc986d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278926} {:answer/id #uuid "c14adf90-e254-5dcb-9985-622bd442251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14adf90-e254-5dcb-9985-622bd442251b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278927} :answer-field/id #uuid "df01f03e-d7e3-5d8c-bea5-b2ed4495539d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c0b5c78-95de-5a23-ba33-d70ce7c6a566" :question/math-academy-id "q-120630" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1)=3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1) & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+4 & =3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{4})=-0.244\\,978≈-0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $-0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi -0.245\\,0≈2.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-0.245\\,0,2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n(-0.245\\,0)\\cdot 2.896\\,6≈-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf6c1221-d440-5800-b355-c06ee44dbbd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278930} {:answer/id #uuid "305dc2c4-9de9-560a-9702-8fd26fb699bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278931} {:answer/id #uuid "73e4049b-0526-5cdb-9368-ad59547fbd21" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278932} {:answer/id #uuid "79557bfb-471a-55fa-bc28-c6edaac725ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278933} {:answer/id #uuid "88af86cd-5842-5adc-b2ca-90810e8d9f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278934}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "305dc2c4-9de9-560a-9702-8fd26fb699bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278931} :answer-field/id #uuid "692a4d96-1725-545a-b185-ffbc6d05c9ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278929}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c5c16c3-3e3c-5e99-b39c-eff2339a9c34" :question/math-academy-id "q-120643" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3=3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =3-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94186dc9-596a-5358-a21a-51bb3c51bc30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94186dc9-596a-5358-a21a-51bb3c51bc30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278937} :answer-field/id #uuid "a28aad4c-866d-5d5f-98bf-766419c5378e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278936} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a0c4676-a83b-5036-8a88-4d79cce83da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278939}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0c4676-a83b-5036-8a88-4d79cce83da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278939} :answer-field/id #uuid "7952590f-7128-5d31-8c58-27c315971474" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c193f82a-da05-5905-aa5f-503b4bedf37c" :question/math-academy-id "q-274558" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}={150}^{∘}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘}+x=2\\cdot {180}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}={330}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={150}^{∘}$ and $x={330}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278940 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2225e952-49f4-5b40-a34c-9c89c1a64feb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "67" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278942} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a232b0-2e2e-5134-b6b6-cdbf8fc819d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "113" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7a232b0-2e2e-5134-b6b6-cdbf8fc819d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "113" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278943} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca1cd32f-d904-56cd-8a73-a993fee0b58b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278941} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c4070b8-8b11-509d-96ca-79f8538aa3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c4070b8-8b11-509d-96ca-79f8538aa3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278945} :answer-field/id #uuid "2919cf91-107b-5187-a5cf-691ce07ed85b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fca07060-bd42-597e-9a34-f938145602be" :question/math-academy-id "q-274562" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-\\frac{3}{7}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{7}{3})=-(66.801\\,4…)^{∘}≈-{66.801}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince $-{66.801}^{∘}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={180}^{∘}+x \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+(-{66.801}^{∘}) \\\\  & =113.199 \\\\  & ≈{113}^{∘}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘}+x \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}+(-{66.801}^{∘}) \\\\  & =293.199 \\\\  & ≈{293}^{∘},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\neach rounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={113}^{∘}$ and $x={293}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278946 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0712c4ec-45ce-5554-a56d-c288e20ff1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278948} {:answer/id #uuid "de66e918-6cf1-5dcf-8eb9-eb8ae53948c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0712c4ec-45ce-5554-a56d-c288e20ff1a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278948} :answer-field/id #uuid "65f24566-d3c7-5af3-ba11-2328b1ea39b0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6b468a-4f69-573b-9bcb-a7b9c9186bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278951}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c6b468a-4f69-573b-9bcb-a7b9c9186bd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278951} :answer-field/id #uuid "55f247c3-51b0-56e8-b8b0-611695eb29a6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b6e07f5-0e84-5942-8281-b6d656744b79" :question/math-academy-id "q-274769" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3=3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+3 & =3+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a23b493-557c-54bb-8c51-427a8e4b5401" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278954} {:answer/id #uuid "243509e1-664b-5f5f-a1f4-36801eca74bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278955} {:answer/id #uuid "41cea4a7-546d-507a-a823-64ebc3c11b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278956} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5a7d2c-042e-58cb-b44d-72b9739d2e7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278957} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd374af-c906-51df-84ac-b73509e1a483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41cea4a7-546d-507a-a823-64ebc3c11b5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278956} :answer-field/id #uuid "77b3799d-89b8-5487-b5d2-78660ea9fb8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "12f917ae-0eb2-5de2-82e7-0cc05d22a03d" :question/math-academy-id "q-29207" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{4}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{16}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "feb606c8-1b16-50c8-97d8-d652064b0290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278961} {:answer/id #uuid "280d9d10-2c5a-5a58-9312-43a70a5108de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278962} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc65731-3185-556d-9ae8-34700ae48384" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278963} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e602d9-e972-5acf-9c8a-f4679aa5f61e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278964} {:answer/id #uuid "d491bab4-2b85-5bba-acd0-16dfdf998469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278965}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d491bab4-2b85-5bba-acd0-16dfdf998469" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278965} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3e931e9-3a4c-5b33-96d9-bba777079da1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9d607ea-fb2b-5d04-99a0-145b3d95ff14" :question/math-academy-id "q-29222" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=-1$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-1)=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8497b40f-7dd9-57c9-af8d-f64edaad0187" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3916" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113163} {:answer/id #uuid "da02af14-b5d9-5bf8-9e2b-9a9a8ac88d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3996" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113164} {:answer/id #uuid "09de61be-4034-56e7-8458-c54c20a6bec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3956" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113165} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3da09a-6e79-546f-b942-a84d539a98a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3936" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113166} {:answer/id #uuid "7669088c-2bab-5c78-948c-a04c784acba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3976" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113167}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da02af14-b5d9-5bf8-9e2b-9a9a8ac88d4a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3996" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113164} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a5db227-9bc7-553a-9332-6fe7b9b26899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186113162}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a1aca94-95fd-5220-bcc5-e0d2edb4ba00" :question/math-academy-id "q-29242" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=11+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =11+\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{11}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{4}{11}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{4}{11})=(19.983\\,1…)^{∘}≈{19.983}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${19.983}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={19.983}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}+{19.983}^{∘}={199.983}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n19.983\\cdot 199.983≈3\\,996\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278966 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e33af4-3af8-5bbf-b0de-3474ded2c3a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,134" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278968} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd0d182-8776-5f6a-a563-0720edfe80f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,564" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278969} {:answer/id #uuid "a37bc472-80c4-56c5-821b-4f9a836332d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278970} {:answer/id #uuid "a498b00d-e812-5ad6-b89a-b6c6083b22ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278971} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4b23c4-c7f8-50a5-881c-9f8de472b86e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278972}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a498b00d-e812-5ad6-b89a-b6c6083b22ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,164" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278971} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d7cf8c2-65b6-55a8-9bcd-b37f374ca4e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "afe96723-09c8-5d86-b142-e57c4c0aa80b" :question/math-academy-id "q-29247" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation  $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\text{arctan}(\\frac{1}{5})=(11.309\\,9…)^{∘}≈{11.310}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${11.310}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={11.310}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{11.310}^{∘}={191.310}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n11.310\\cdot 191.310≈2\\,164\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7663d36c-360a-501b-8ebb-54a3ae9f9bb7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278975} {:answer/id #uuid "25ebf019-5378-5857-81c5-6e11693695be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278976} {:answer/id #uuid "27e11647-1c67-579d-a78b-2ce23fb366dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278977} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ab013-c9c8-541a-bdcf-9b36e1a2e95d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278978} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2933fc-aa05-5bf7-ae2a-e4bcdac8b898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb2933fc-aa05-5bf7-ae2a-e4bcdac8b898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278979} :answer-field/id #uuid "52c9483b-1927-563e-aeee-f70a6605b9d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fe37897-32fe-50a9-97e1-fcfe920775fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-29692" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+2 & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+22 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =20 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{4})=0.244\\,978≈0.245\\,0\n$$\n\nSince $0.245\\,0$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.245\\,0.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +0.245\\,0=-2.896\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=0.245\\,0,-2.896\\,6.$\n\nFinally, the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n0.245\\,0\\cdot (-2.896\\,6)≈-0.7\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278980 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2a3fd83-297b-55bf-99b5-24bf187c64dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278982} {:answer/id #uuid "acdb7989-4fe0-5d4d-a819-552e91d17967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acdb7989-4fe0-5d4d-a819-552e91d17967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278983} :answer-field/id #uuid "5975a918-9dd1-5a91-9bb8-9b3ba13be609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eda058f7-6eeb-5bc9-a889-86b106b473ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda058f7-6eeb-5bc9-a889-86b106b473ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278985} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f0a89c4-4972-5a78-9bbf-678456cdabd5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a0fadc36-cf82-5474-9255-b11413dca9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-326456" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={30}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}={210}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba963ded-e9ff-5324-9d22-acb6b7a85e10" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a3b1cad-960f-5b95-8c49-b3f466a081cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a3b1cad-960f-5b95-8c49-b3f466a081cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278988} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fd6f169-aef9-540e-b3ab-c87e372c8b5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9186927d-9595-5c2e-acd1-153523445455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9186927d-9595-5c2e-acd1-153523445455" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278990} :answer-field/id #uuid "b18be829-c5e5-53d1-b794-4433f333a7a0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "94fb70fb-6693-5842-96d8-53b4983d8a14" :question/math-academy-id "q-327067" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}} and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{3}{-\\sqrt{3}}=-\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{arctan}(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\pi +x=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}, \\\\ {x}_{2} & =2\\pi +x=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca7da035-d6df-5764-96a7-84fccc1bbd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278993} {:answer/id #uuid "1c5070b6-d21e-5ddb-9e65-9b21b37d7b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ca7da035-d6df-5764-96a7-84fccc1bbd3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278993} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b596d55-605d-513d-893e-12400ae08475" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efa5c6da-b3b1-51f3-8678-79720081a3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efa5c6da-b3b1-51f3-8678-79720081a3a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "256" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278996} :answer-field/id #uuid "358d68eb-de0d-5dba-a947-62968792248f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5dd12e1b-3e10-56f5-a9b3-6cdf51c48527" :question/math-academy-id "q-327780" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{4}$  for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(4)=(75.963\\,7…)^{∘}≈{75.964}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${75.964}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is ${x}_{1}={76}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={180}^{∘}+x={180}^{∘}+{75.964}^{∘}≈{256}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={76}^{∘}$ and $x={256}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c71c49ee-e496-5d4e-87fa-96e28dd830ca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b80a356c-327e-5265-8518-cfcad5687e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b80a356c-327e-5265-8518-cfcad5687e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278999} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2bc3b6f-eba2-50a1-b7b7-fdaf6fc8e501" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68ab632d-06ae-55c1-b6b9-5398225dd1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68ab632d-06ae-55c1-b6b9-5398225dd1a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279001} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d45779a-f730-5b74-a378-80c55503ddb7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d33a131c-42fa-5ae5-84f9-b33cd3339f50" :question/math-academy-id "q-327790" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{5}{4}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}} and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{5})=0.674\\,741…≈0.674\\,7\n$$\n\nSince $0.674\\,7$ radians lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is ${x}_{1}=0.7.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\pi +x=\\pi +0.674\\,7≈3.8,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=0.7$ and $x=3.8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53fdeea6-c18f-50d8-8e90-bc5228ed3f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279004} {:answer/id #uuid "914e68ff-bbf1-5a1d-917c-5eaf66e852b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fdeea6-c18f-50d8-8e90-bc5228ed3f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279004} :answer-field/id #uuid "48b437ce-b0ef-5eb9-8a7f-0e44fa034843" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92072a3c-e596-52f1-bc87-2ec14ae5396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92072a3c-e596-52f1-bc87-2ec14ae5396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a5b793a-70ed-5351-9cf0-1fa18289bf4d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "817e1b19-74f8-5646-ae5c-716cd762b4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-330672" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to one decimal place**, the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1=-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+13$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order**, the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-1 & =-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+13 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =14 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{2}{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{7})=0.278\\,2…≈0.278\n$$\n\nSince $0.278$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to one decimal place, is ${x}_{1}=0.3.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add and subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\pi +x=-\\pi +0.278≈-2.9,\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=-2.9$ and $x=0.3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c9c0f6-034c-5d9c-935a-ca27dec1a648" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Cotangent Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f00ad69-352f-5e00-b08f-445f43c82540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279010} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5f5ec2-56ae-56aa-a673-985d7696cbb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f00ad69-352f-5e00-b08f-445f43c82540" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279010} :answer-field/id #uuid "088dba68-4ced-5d6b-a917-93fd475929a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bab79a72-753e-54a0-b798-1e547e397f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bab79a72-753e-54a0-b798-1e547e397f31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279013} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae1b57bd-9066-5ee1-9d82-0f80c2f0ee14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "937d6843-1044-57f7-9c8a-53013d229225" :question/math-academy-id "q-330688" :question/problem "Find, **in ascending order and rounded to the nearest integer**, the two solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+6$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<{180}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$ \n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+6 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =6 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=(26.565\\,0…)^{∘}≈{26.565}^{∘}\n$$\n\nSince ${26.565}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution, rounded to the nearest integer, is ${x}_{1}={27}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-{180}^{∘}+x=-{180}^{∘}+{26.565}^{∘}≈-{153}^{∘},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x={-153}^{∘}$ and $x={27}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4d0b1a-2390-539c-b86a-bd8f1d3b3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a4d0b1a-2390-539c-b86a-bd8f1d3b3a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279016} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5458c8-33ea-50f8-b914-294bdb03eaa3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec37de83-979e-562b-9b95-7288a19c6666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec37de83-979e-562b-9b95-7288a19c6666" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279018} :answer-field/id #uuid "77865180-5e60-592d-96dd-f7706d106ef4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52aca61e-02c9-5f63-9795-8391b2d85977" :question/math-academy-id "q-330707" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for $0<x<2\\pi .$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}\nand $x=$ \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec326b1e-7e40-53f6-ab89-e9ca255b3bc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "180" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279021} {:answer/id #uuid "1744a427-773d-5213-bf4e-d512f4c44454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1744a427-773d-5213-bf4e-d512f4c44454" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279022} :answer-field/id #uuid "784d2ce2-b722-5437-8bcc-6fdb857b977d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83e5412b-a9ee-58bf-9752-01d0fb1baa47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83e5412b-a9ee-58bf-9752-01d0fb1baa47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "270" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279024} :answer-field/id #uuid "c57a8a7a-0c9e-5503-9a92-9aeba9a30349" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78f73c58-6327-5d33-99f1-a45e67770ab5" :question/math-academy-id "q-330722" :question/problem "Find the two solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n**In ascending order,** the solutions are $x=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$ and $x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\sqrt{18} & =3\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{18} \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =x+{180}^{∘}={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={270}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45cbbc7e-3a36-5410-8b17-df56d478cf15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279027} {:answer/id #uuid "fb12cba3-85e2-5421-974a-3c533347c99f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279028} {:answer/id #uuid "202ea43e-84a2-5b61-afad-7c6680129da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279029} {:answer/id #uuid "4610b5fa-7afb-5ab3-ab8c-dc9b551e5eba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279030} {:answer/id #uuid "e40d8568-be74-5186-896d-3202579ef4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40d8568-be74-5186-896d-3202579ef4f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279031} :answer-field/id #uuid "80531478-53c0-5051-b248-4468d02db2b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2d36579-fcf3-58d1-882f-1fb91ac58539" :question/math-academy-id "q-80200" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5=-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\operatorname{cot}⁡x-5 & =-5-3\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we subtract integer multiples of $\\pi $ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}.\n$$"}]}
